  "text": "A tutor employs weight quantization after training to store model parameters with fewer bits, thereby reducing the memory required to store those parameters. However, an aggressive reduction in precision may degrade answer quality. The tutor also employs retrieval augmented generation after training for different purposes; when the tutor answers a question, this technique supplies relevant course passages. A retrieval encoder maps questions and document passages into embeddings, which similarity search uses to select candidate passages from an index. These selected passages are added to the language models context. Updating the index enables making revised course material available without retraining the language model. Retrieval itself does not change the models parameters. Additionally, a highly ranked passage can still be irrelevant content.",
  "reason": "The text was constructed by sequentially linking concepts via their defined relations in source occurrence order: tutor -> quantization techniques (weight quantization, RAG) -> effects on storage/memory/quality -> RAG mechanism (encoder -> search -> selection -> context) -> index update benefits -> retrieval property (no parameter change) -> edge case (ranked passage irrelevance). All argument qualifiers (e.g., 'after training', 'with fewer bits', 'may', 'does not') and modality were preserved as specified in the relation definitions."
